From: h713 <h713@local>
Date: Mon, 1 Sep 2026 22:40:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] misc: decd: flip the IOMMU with the video source parked

Patch 0075 enables IOMMU master-2 translation inside the first frame submit.
That works, but only if the DECD video source happens to be disabled at that
moment, and 0075 gives no such guarantee: dec_frame_submit() is precisely the
path reachable with the source already enabled by whatever set up the display
route.

The AFBD video source rests at base address 0x00000000 with an inherited
1920x1088 / stride-1920 geometry, so while it is enabled it is raster-scanning
from physical address 0 across roughly the first 2 MB. Under bypass those reads
are harmless, which is why the DECD route always worked on a bypass boot. The
instant master 2 starts translating they are L1-invalid, and one master-2 fault
wedges the AFBD fetch engine for the rest of the boot.

Measured on hardware 2026-09-01. Flipping with the source enabled faulted 4 of
4 times, at 0x29000, 0x26000, 0x81000 and 0x16000 -- page-aligned, different
every run (a scan caught at whatever offset it had reached), all inside that
first 2 MB, and never near the identity-mapped logo carveout at 0x6c100000 or
the frame IOVA at 0xfe000000. Flipping with only the inherited logo route live
faulted zero times, and a following visible session played 300 moving frames at
27.13 fps from a Cedrus buffer with 56 physical breaks and a 32 KiB longest run.

So park the source across the transition: clear source 0's enable bits, pulse
the commit latch, flip, and re-enable afterwards. Clearing bits 1:0 of AFBD
+0x10 and pulsing the latch at +0x14 is a measured way to stop the fetch rather
than an inference -- on stock that pair replaced playing video with solid green
(zero luma) and reverted cleanly, while the identical write *without* the latch
pulse was completely inert, landing and reading back but holding the picture
unchanged for 300 samples over six seconds.

The subtlety is *when* to re-enable, and two measured failures pinned it down.

A first version restored the source immediately after the flip and faulted at
exactly 0x00000000, seventeen milliseconds after an otherwise clean transition:
the frame's address is not programmed that early, so re-enabling merely
restarted the same scan from base 0, now under translation. The fault address
moving from a mid-scan value to precisely zero is what identified it.

A second version moved the restore after the enqueue and gated it on the Y base
becoming non-zero. That ran with zero faults but timed out and left the source
parked -- and a post-run read then found all four Y slots holding 0xFDC00000.
The base was never late; the poll was in the wrong place. dec_reg_enable() at
the end of dec_frame_submit() is what kicks the block into consuming the queue,
and it runs after the enqueue, so the ring cannot be armed before it.

So unpark after dec_reg_enable(), and gate on any of the four Y ring slots being
non-zero -- waiting on slot 0 alone would be a guess about which slot the first
frame lands in, and a wrong guess is indistinguishable from "no base arrived".
If none ever arrives, leave the source parked and warn: a blank panel costs a
restore, a master-2 fault costs the whole boot. That safety gate has already
earned itself once, turning what would have been a second wedged boot into a
warning line and a clean recovery.

The failure path restores immediately, which is safe because
sun50i_iommu_set_master_translation() only reports -EIO when the bypass bit is
still set, so the master is still bypassing and a scan from base 0 cannot fault.

HARDWARE-VALIDATED 2026-09-01, as the first DECD session of a boot with no
operator procedure of any kind:

  - visible frozen frame 60: zero faults, "video source unparked onto
    0xfe000000 after 75 us", operator-confirmed still, logo restored;
  - visible moving playback: zero faults, 300 frames at 27.12 fps, 299 fence
    retirements, zero stalls, operator-confirmed animation, logo restored.

The 75 us is worth keeping: the ring arms on the first poll iteration once
dec_reg_enable() has run, which is why the v2 poll could never have succeeded at
any timeout -- it was in the wrong place, not too short.

In the moving run no park was needed at all, because the diagnostic wrapper was
also fixed to wait for the ring before enabling the source, so the source was
already disabled when the flip happened. That is the better arrangement and this
patch becomes the safety net for callers that do not do it. Both belong in
place: userspace should not enable a source with no frame behind it, and the
driver should not assume userspace got that right.

(One comment was corrected after the validated build -- it described the unpark
point as "once a frame has been enqueued", the exact rule that was wrong in v2.
Comment text only; the tested object is otherwise identical.) It should make the two-step bench
procedure (spend the flip with a nonvisible run, then run the visible test)
unnecessary; until a visible run confirms that, keep using the procedure, which
is proven. Applies on top of the out-of-series 0075 and shares its out-of-series
status.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_ioctl.c b/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_ioctl.c
--- a/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_ioctl.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_ioctl.c
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
 
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/sun50i-iommu.h>
 #include <linux/uaccess.h>
@@ -45,11 +46,151 @@
  *   - mutex_unlock(a1)
  *   - always returns 0
  */
+
+/*
+ * Source 0's control word, its commit latch, and its Y base address.  A write
+ * to the AFBD block does nothing until the latch is pulsed: an uncommitted
+ * write lands, reads back and holds forever while remaining inert, so readback
+ * alone proves nothing here.
+ */
+#define DECD_AFBD_SRC0_CTRL	0x10
+#define DECD_AFBD_SRC0_CTRL_EN	GENMASK(1, 0)
+#define DECD_AFBD_SRC0_COMMIT	0x14
+#define DECD_AFBD_SRC0_Y_RING	0x70
+#define DECD_AFBD_SRC0_Y_SLOTS	4
+
+/*
+ * The latch is self-clearing: the hardware consumes the bit, so reading back 0
+ * is success and reading back 1 means it was not taken.  Measured well under a
+ * millisecond; the bound only stops us spinning forever if the block is wedged.
+ */
+static void dec_afbd_commit(void __iomem *afbd)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	writel(1, afbd + DECD_AFBD_SRC0_COMMIT);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
+		if (!readl(afbd + DECD_AFBD_SRC0_COMMIT))
+			return;
+		udelay(10);
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * Park the AFBD video source across the bypass->translation transition.
+ *
+ * The source sits at base 0 with an inherited 1920x1088 geometry until a frame
+ * programs it, so while it is enabled it is scanning the bottom of physical
+ * memory.  That is harmless under bypass and an immediate L1-invalid fault
+ * under translation, and one master-2 fault wedges the AFBD fetch engine for
+ * the rest of the boot.
+ *
+ * On success the source is left PARKED and *parked is set: the caller must call
+ * dec_afbd_unpark_source() after dec_reg_enable(), because re-enabling
+ * it any earlier just restarts the same scan from base 0 under translation.
+ * That is not hypothetical -- doing exactly that faulted at 0x00000000,
+ * seventeen milliseconds after an otherwise clean flip.
+ *
+ * On failure the flip did not take (sun50i_iommu_set_master_translation only
+ * reports -EIO when the bypass bit is still set), so the master is still
+ * bypassing and restoring immediately is safe.
+ */
+static int dec_iommu_enable_translation(struct dec_device *dec, u32 *saved_ctrl,
+					bool *parked)
+{
+	void __iomem *afbd = dec->regs ? dec->regs->afbd : NULL;
+	u32 ctrl = 0;
+	int ret;
+
+	*parked = false;
+	*saved_ctrl = 0;
+
+	if (!afbd) {
+		dev_warn(dec->dev,
+			 "no AFBD mapping; enabling translation without parking the source\n");
+		return sun50i_iommu_set_master_translation(dec->dev, true);
+	}
+
+	ctrl = readl(afbd + DECD_AFBD_SRC0_CTRL);
+	if (ctrl & DECD_AFBD_SRC0_CTRL_EN) {
+		writel(ctrl & ~DECD_AFBD_SRC0_CTRL_EN, afbd + DECD_AFBD_SRC0_CTRL);
+		dec_afbd_commit(afbd);
+		*saved_ctrl = ctrl;
+		*parked = true;
+	}
+
+	ret = sun50i_iommu_set_master_translation(dec->dev, true);
+	if (ret && *parked) {
+		writel(ctrl, afbd + DECD_AFBD_SRC0_CTRL);
+		dec_afbd_commit(afbd);
+		*parked = false;
+	}
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Re-enable the video source, but only once the hardware has a base address to
+ * fetch from.  Gate on the Y ring rather than assuming it is programmed by the
+ * time we get here: the ring is filled asynchronously once dec_reg_enable()
+ * kicks the block, so an unconditional restore would only move the fault rather
+ * than remove it.
+ *
+ * Any of the four slots counts.  Waiting on slot 0 alone would be a guess about
+ * which slot the first frame lands in, and a wrong guess here is indistinguish-
+ * able from "the base never arrived".
+ *
+ * If no base ever arrives, leave the source parked.  A blank panel costs a
+ * restore; a master-2 fault costs the whole boot.
+ */
+static bool dec_afbd_ring_armed(void __iomem *afbd, u32 *base)
+{
+	int slot;
+
+	for (slot = 0; slot < DECD_AFBD_SRC0_Y_SLOTS; slot++) {
+		u32 v = readl(afbd + DECD_AFBD_SRC0_Y_RING + slot * 4);
+
+		if (v) {
+			*base = v;
+			return true;
+		}
+	}
+	return false;
+}
+
+static void dec_afbd_unpark_source(struct dec_device *dec, u32 ctrl)
+{
+	void __iomem *afbd = dec->regs ? dec->regs->afbd : NULL;
+	u32 base = 0;
+	int i;
+
+	if (!afbd)
+		return;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 5000; i++) {
+		if (dec_afbd_ring_armed(afbd, &base)) {
+			writel(ctrl, afbd + DECD_AFBD_SRC0_CTRL);
+			dec_afbd_commit(afbd);
+			dev_info(dec->dev,
+				 "video source unparked onto %#x after %d us\n",
+				 base, i * 75);
+			return;
+		}
+		usleep_range(50, 100);
+	}
+
+	dev_warn(dec->dev,
+		 "video source has no base address after enqueue; leaving it parked\n");
+}
+
 int dec_frame_submit(struct dec_device *dec,
 		     struct dec_frame_submit_desc *desc,
 		     int *release_fence_fd, int repeat)
 {
 	struct dec_frame_item *item;
+	bool parked = false;
+	u32 src_ctrl = 0;
 	int ret = 0;
 
 	if (!dec || !desc)
@@ -68,8 +209,8 @@
 		item = frame_item_create(dec, desc);
 		if (item) {
 			if (!dec->iommu_runtime_enabled) {
-				ret = sun50i_iommu_set_master_translation(dec->dev,
-								      true);
+				ret = dec_iommu_enable_translation(dec, &src_ctrl,
+								   &parked);
 				if (ret) {
 					dev_err(dec->dev,
 						"cannot enable runtime IOMMU translation: %d\n",
@@ -98,6 +239,16 @@
 	}
 	dec_reg_blue_en(dec->regs, desc->blue_en);
 	dec_reg_enable(dec->regs, true);
+
+	/*
+	 * dec_reg_enable() above is what kicks the hardware into consuming the
+	 * queue, so this is the earliest point at which the ring can hold a real
+	 * address -- an earlier attempt polled before this call and timed out
+	 * with all four slots still zero, then found them all programmed once
+	 * the run finished.
+	 */
+	if (parked)
+		dec_afbd_unpark_source(dec, src_ctrl);
 out:
 	mutex_unlock(&dec->lock);
 	return ret;
